<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-graph-theory">
<title>Graph Theory</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Sets, relations, functions, basic counting, discrete structures,
recurrence relations, matrices, and elementary proof techniques.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>define graphs, vertices, edges, and adjacency;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish simple graphs, multigraphs, pseudographs,
          directed graphs, and weighted graphs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute vertex degrees and apply the handshaking lemma;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with paths, walks, trails, circuits, and cycles;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine connectedness and connected components;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify complete, bipartite, multipartite, regular, and
          planar graphs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with graph complements and graph isomorphisms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>represent graphs using adjacency and incidence matrices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand subgraphs and induced subgraphs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify trees, forests, and spanning trees;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply basic tree properties;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Euler trails and Euler circuits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Hamiltonian paths and cycles;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with graph colorings and chromatic number;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand matchings and vertex covers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>state Hall's Marriage Theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize graph connectivity parameters;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand planar embeddings and Euler's planar formula;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize graph algorithms such as BFS and DFS;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect graph theory with networks, optimization, probability,
          algebra, and combinatorics.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-what-is-a-graph">
<title>What Is a Graph?</title>
<p>A graph is a mathematical structure used to represent pairwise
relationships among objects.</p>

<definition>
<title>Graph</title>
<statement>
<p>A graph is an ordered pair</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G=(V,E),
}</md>
</p>

<p>where <m>V</m> is a set of vertices and <m>E</m> is a collection of edges
joining pairs of vertices.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The set <m>V</m> is called the <em>vertex set</em>.</p>

<p>The set <m>E</m> is called the <em>edge set</em>.</p>

<p>The symbols</p>

<p>
<md>|V|</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>|E|</md>
</p>

<p>denote the number of vertices and edges.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-vertices-and-edges">
<title>Vertices and Edges</title>
<p>A vertex represents an object.</p>

<p>An edge represents a relationship between two objects.</p>

<p>For example, if</p>

<p>
<md>V=\{A,B,C,D\},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>E=
\{
\{A,B\},
\{A,C\},
\{B,D\}
\},</md>
</p>

<p>then the graph contains four vertices and three edges.</p>

<p>Two vertices joined by an edge are called adjacent.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-simple-graphs">
<title>Simple Graphs</title>
<definition>
<title>Simple Graph</title>
<statement>
<p>A simple graph is an undirected graph with:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>no loops;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>no multiple edges between the same pair of vertices.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus every edge in a simple graph is an unordered pair</p>

<p>
<md>\{u,v\}</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>u\neq v.</md>
</p>

<p>Most of this section focuses on finite simple graphs unless otherwise
stated.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-multigraphs-pseudographs">
<title>Multigraphs and Pseudographs</title>
<p>A multigraph may contain multiple edges joining the same pair of
vertices.</p>

<p>A pseudograph may additionally contain loops.</p>

<p>A loop is an edge joining a vertex to itself.</p>

<p>These generalized graph models are useful when pairwise relationships
may occur more than once.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>transportation systems with several routes between cities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>electrical networks with parallel connections;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>communication systems with multiple channels.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-directed-graphs">
<title>Directed Graphs</title>
<p>A directed graph, or digraph, assigns an orientation to each edge.</p>

<p>A directed edge is an ordered pair</p>

<p>
<md>(u,v).</md>
</p>

<p>It is commonly written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
u\longrightarrow v.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The direction matters.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>(u,v)\neq(v,u)</md>
</p>

<p>in general.</p>

<p>Directed graphs model asymmetric relationships such as:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>web links;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>prerequisite structures;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>one-way roads;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>information flow;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>dominance relations.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-weighted-graphs">
<title>Weighted Graphs</title>
<p>A weighted graph assigns a numerical value to each edge.</p>

<p>A weight function may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
w:E\to\mathbb{R}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The value <m>w(e)</m> may represent:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>distance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>travel time;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>cost;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>capacity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>reliability;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>energy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Weighted graphs are fundamental in optimization and network analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-drawings">
<title>Graph Drawings</title>
<p>A graph may be drawn by representing vertices as points and edges as
curves joining them.</p>

<p>The drawing is not the graph itself.</p>

<p>Two very different drawings may represent the same abstract graph.</p>

<p>For example, crossing edges in a drawing do not automatically create a
new vertex.</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
A graph is determined by its vertex-edge relationships, not by the
specific geometric placement used in a drawing.
\end{importantbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-degree-of-vertex">
<title>Degree of a Vertex</title>
<definition>
<title>Degree</title>
<statement>
<p>The degree of a vertex <m>v</m>, written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(v),
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the number of edges incident with <m>v</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>In a simple graph, this is also the number of neighbors of <m>v</m>.</p>

<p>A vertex of degree <m>0</m> is called isolated.</p>

<p>A vertex of degree <m>1</m> is often called a leaf or pendant vertex,
especially in a tree.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-neighborhood">
<title>Neighborhood</title>
<p>The open neighborhood of <m>v</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
N(v)
=
\{u\in V:\{u,v\}\in E\}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The closed neighborhood is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
N[v]
=
N(v)\cup\{v\}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a simple graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(v)=|N(v)|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Neighborhoods are central in local graph analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-handshaking-lemma">
<title>Handshaking Lemma</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Handshaking Lemma]
For every finite undirected graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)
=
2|E|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Every edge contributes exactly <m>2</m> to the total degree sum because it
has two endpoints.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-worked-handshaking-lemma">
<title>Worked Example: Handshaking Lemma</title>
<example>
<title>Finding the Number of Edges</title>
<p>Suppose a graph has vertex degrees</p>

<p>
<md>4,3,3,2,2,2.</md>
</p>

<p>The total degree is</p>

<p>
<md>4+3+3+2+2+2=16.</md>
</p>

<p>By the handshaking lemma,</p>

<p>
<md>2|E|=16.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|=8.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-odd-degree-vertices">
<title>Odd-Degree Vertices</title>
<p>A direct consequence of the handshaking lemma is:</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
Every finite undirected graph has an even number of vertices of odd
degree.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)</md>
</p>

<p>is even, the number of odd summands must be even.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-regular-graphs">
<title>Regular Graphs</title>
<definition>
<title>Regular Graph</title>
<statement>
<p>A graph is <m>r</m>-regular if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(v)=r
}</md>
</p>

<p>for every vertex <m>v</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>If <m>G</m> is <m>r</m>-regular on <m>n</m> vertices, then</p>

<p>
<md>nr=2|E|.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|=\frac{nr}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In particular, <m>nr</m> must be even.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-complete-graphs">
<title>Complete Graphs</title>
<p>The complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Every pair of distinct vertices is adjacent.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\deg(v)=n-1</md>
</p>

<p>for every vertex.</p>

<p>The number of edges is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E(K_n)|
=
\binom{n}{2}
=
\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bipartite-graphs">
<title>Bipartite Graphs</title>
<definition>
<title>Bipartite Graph</title>
<statement>
<p>A graph is bipartite if its vertex set can be partitioned into two
disjoint sets</p>

<p>
<md>V=X\cup Y</md>
</p>

<p>such that every edge has one endpoint in <m>X</m> and one endpoint in
<m>Y</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>There are no edges joining two vertices within the same part.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-complete-bipartite-graphs">
<title>Complete Bipartite Graphs</title>
<p>The complete bipartite graph with part sizes <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_{m,n}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Every vertex in the first part is joined to every vertex in the second.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E(K_{m,n})|=mn.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bipartite-characterization">
<title>Characterization of Bipartite Graphs</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}
A graph is bipartite if and only if it contains no odd cycle.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus the existence of an odd cycle is exactly the obstruction to
bipartiteness.</p>

<p>This theorem connects local cycle structure with a global vertex
partition.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-multipartite-graphs">
<title>Multipartite Graphs</title>
<p>A graph is <m>r</m>-partite if its vertex set can be divided into <m>r</m>
parts so that no edge joins two vertices in the same part.</p>

<p>A complete <m>r</m>-partite graph contains every possible edge between
different parts.</p>

<p>Tur\'an graphs introduced in
Section <xref ref="sec-extremal-combinatorics" />
are balanced complete multipartite graphs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-subgraphs">
<title>Subgraphs</title>
<definition>
<title>Subgraph</title>
<statement>
<p>A graph <m>H</m> is a subgraph of <m>G</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>V(H)\subseteq V(G)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>E(H)\subseteq E(G).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A subgraph may omit vertices, edges, or both.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-induced-subgraphs">
<title>Induced Subgraphs</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>S\subseteq V(G),</md>
</p>

<p>the induced subgraph</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G[S]
}</md>
</p>

<p>contains vertex set <m>S</m> and every edge of <m>G</m> whose endpoints both
lie in <m>S</m>.</p>

<p>Thus an induced subgraph is determined entirely by its chosen vertex
set.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-spanning-subgraphs">
<title>Spanning Subgraphs</title>
<p>A spanning subgraph has the same vertex set as the original graph:</p>

<p>
<md>V(H)=V(G).</md>
</p>

<p>It may contain only some of the edges.</p>

<p>Spanning trees are especially important examples.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-complement">
<title>Graph Complement</title>
<p>For a simple graph <m>G</m>, its complement is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\overline{G}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Two distinct vertices are adjacent in <m>\overline{G}</m> exactly when
they are not adjacent in <m>G</m>.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
E(G)\cap E(\overline{G})=\varnothing.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E(G)|+|E(\overline{G})|
=
\binom{|V|}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-isomorphism">
<title>Graph Isomorphism</title>
<definition>
<title>Graph Isomorphism</title>
<statement>
<p>Graphs <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> are isomorphic if there exists a bijection</p>

<p>
<md>f:V(G)\to V(H)</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\{u,v\}\in E(G)
\iff
\{f(u),f(v)\}\in E(H).
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>An isomorphism preserves adjacency.</p>

<p>Isomorphic graphs have the same abstract structure even if they are
drawn differently.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-invariants">
<title>Graph Invariants</title>
<p>A graph invariant is a property preserved under isomorphism.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>number of vertices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>number of edges;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>degree sequence;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>number of connected components;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>cycle lengths;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>chromatic number;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>adjacency spectrum.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Different values of an invariant prove non-isomorphism.</p>

<p>Matching invariants do not necessarily prove isomorphism.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-degree-sequences">
<title>Degree Sequences</title>
<p>The degree sequence of a graph is the list of vertex degrees, typically
written in nonincreasing order.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(4,3,3,2,2,0)
}</md>
</p>

<p>is a possible degree sequence.</p>

<p>A natural question is whether a given integer sequence is
<em>graphical</em>, meaning that some simple graph has exactly that
degree sequence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-havel-hakimi">
<title>Havel--Hakimi Principle</title>
<p>The Havel--Hakimi procedure tests graphical degree sequences.</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>d_1\geq d_2\geq\cdots\geq d_n.</md>
</p>

<p>Remove <m>d_1</m> and subtract <m>1</m> from the next <m>d_1</m> entries.</p>

<p>The original sequence is graphical if and only if the resulting
sequence is graphical, provided the reduction remains valid.</p>

<p>This gives a recursive algorithmic test.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-walks">
<title>Walks</title>
<p>A walk is a sequence</p>

<p>
<md>v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_k</md>
</p>

<p>such that consecutive vertices are adjacent.</p>

<p>The length of the walk is</p>

<p>
<md>k.</md>
</p>

<p>Vertices and edges may repeat.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-trails">
<title>Trails</title>
<p>A trail is a walk in which no edge is repeated.</p>

<p>Vertices may still repeat.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{trail}
\Longrightarrow
\text{walk}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The converse need not hold.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-paths">
<title>Paths</title>
<p>A path is a walk in which no vertex is repeated.</p>

<p>A path with <m>k</m> edges has <m>k+1</m> vertices.</p>

<p>If a path begins at <m>u</m> and ends at <m>v</m>, it is called a
<m>u</m>-<m>v</m> path.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-closed-walks-circuits">
<title>Closed Walks and Circuits</title>
<p>A closed walk begins and ends at the same vertex.</p>

<p>A circuit is a closed trail.</p>

<p>A cycle is a closed path except that the first and last vertex are the
same.</p>

<p>Thus a cycle contains no repeated vertices other than its initial/final
vertex.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-cycles">
<title>Cycles</title>
<p>The cycle graph on <m>n</m> vertices is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
C_n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Every vertex in <m>C_n</m> has degree <m>2</m>.</p>

<p>The graph contains exactly one cycle using all <m>n</m> vertices.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-distance">
<title>Distance</title>
<p>The distance between vertices <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>, written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d(u,v),
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the length of a shortest <m>u</m>-<m>v</m> path.</p>

<p>For vertices in the same connected component, graph distance defines a
metric.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>d(u,v)\geq0,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>d(u,v)=d(v,u),</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>d(u,w)\leq d(u,v)+d(v,w).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-eccentricity">
<title>Eccentricity</title>
<p>For a connected graph, the eccentricity of <m>v</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{ecc}(v)
=
\max_{u\in V}d(u,v).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This measures the greatest distance from <m>v</m> to any other vertex.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-diameter-radius">
<title>Diameter and Radius</title>
<p>The diameter of a connected graph is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{diam}(G)
=
\max_{u,v\in V}d(u,v).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The radius is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{rad}(G)
=
\min_{v\in V}\operatorname{ecc}(v).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Vertices attaining the radius form the center of the graph.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-connected-graphs">
<title>Connected Graphs</title>
<definition>
<title>Connected Graph</title>
<statement>
<p>A graph is connected if every pair of vertices is joined by a path.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>If no such path exists for some pair, the graph is disconnected.</p>

<p>Connectivity is one of the most fundamental structural properties of a
graph.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-connected-components">
<title>Connected Components</title>
<p>A connected component is a maximal connected subgraph.</p>

<p>Every graph decomposes uniquely into connected components.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G
=
G_1\cup G_2\cup\cdots\cup G_k
}</md>
</p>

<p>where each <m>G_i</m> is connected and the components have disjoint vertex
sets.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-cut-vertices">
<title>Cut Vertices</title>
<p>A cut vertex is a vertex whose removal increases the number of connected
components.</p>

<p>Cut vertices represent structurally critical points in a network.</p>

<p>They are also called articulation vertices.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bridges">
<title>Bridges</title>
<p>A bridge is an edge whose removal increases the number of connected
components.</p>

<p>Equivalently, an edge is a bridge if and only if it lies on no cycle.</p>

<p>Thus cycles provide alternate routes around edges.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-vertex-edge-connectivity">
<title>Vertex and Edge Connectivity</title>
<p>The vertex connectivity of a graph is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa(G).
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is the minimum number of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph
or reduces it to a trivial graph.</p>

<p>The edge connectivity is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lambda(G).
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is the minimum number of edges whose removal disconnects the graph.</p>

<p>For many nontrivial connected graphs,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa(G)\leq\lambda(G)\leq\delta(G),
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\delta(G)</md>
</p>

<p>is the minimum degree.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-adjacency-matrices">
<title>Adjacency Matrices</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>V=\{v_1,\ldots,v_n\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The adjacency matrix of a simple graph is</p>

<p>
<md>A=(a_{ij}),</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a_{ij}
=
\begin{cases}
1,&amp;v_i\sim v_j,\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here,</p>

<p>
<md>v_i\sim v_j</md>
</p>

<p>means <m>v_i</m> is adjacent to <m>v_j</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-adjacency-matrix-properties">
<title>Properties of Adjacency Matrices</title>
<p>For a finite simple undirected graph:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><m>A</m> is symmetric;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>diagonal entries are <m>0</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>row sums equal vertex degrees.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(v_i)
=
\sum_{j=1}^{n}a_{ij}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-powers-adjacency-matrix">
<title>Powers of the Adjacency Matrix</title>
<p>A fundamental fact is:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(A^k)_{ij}
=
\text{number of walks of length }k
\text{ from }v_i\text{ to }v_j.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This creates a powerful connection between graph theory and linear
algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-worked-adjacency-matrix">
<title>Worked Example: Adjacency Matrix</title>
<example>
<title>A Three-Vertex Path</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>P_3:
\qquad
v_1-v_2-v_3.</md>
</p>

<p>Its adjacency matrix is</p>

<p>
<md>A=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
1&amp;0&amp;1\\
0&amp;1&amp;0
\end{pmatrix}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>A^2=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&amp;0&amp;1\\
0&amp;2&amp;0\\
1&amp;0&amp;1
\end{pmatrix}.</md>
</p>

<p>The entry</p>

<p>
<md>(A^2)_{13}=1</md>
</p>

<p>shows there is exactly one walk of length <m>2</m> from <m>v_1</m> to <m>v_3</m>.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
v_1\to v_2\to v_3
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the unique length-<m>2</m> walk from <m>v_1</m> to <m>v_3</m>.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-incidence-matrices">
<title>Incidence Matrices</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>V=\{v_1,\ldots,v_n\}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>E=\{e_1,\ldots,e_m\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The incidence matrix</p>

<p>
<md>B=(b_{ij})</md>
</p>

<p>has</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
b_{ij}
=
\begin{cases}
1,&amp;v_i\text{ is incident with }e_j,\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a simple undirected graph, each column contains exactly two <m>1</m>'s.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-trees">
<title>Trees</title>
<definition>
<title>Tree</title>
<statement>
<p>A tree is a connected graph containing no cycles.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Trees are among the most important graph structures.</p>

<p>They describe hierarchy, branching, and minimal connectivity.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-tree-characterizations">
<title>Equivalent Characterizations of Trees</title>
<p>For a finite graph <m>T</m> with <m>n</m> vertices, the following are
equivalent:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><m>T</m> is a tree;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>T</m> is connected and acyclic;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>T</m> is connected and has <m>n-1</m> edges;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>T</m> is acyclic and has <m>n-1</m> edges;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>there is exactly one path between every pair of vertices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>every edge is a bridge.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These equivalent descriptions make trees unusually flexible to study.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-tree-edge-count">
<title>Number of Edges in a Tree</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}
Every tree with <m>n</m> vertices has exactly</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
n-1
}</md>
</p>

<p>edges.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This may be proved by induction using the existence of leaves.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-tree-leaves">
<title>Leaves in Trees</title>
<p>Every finite tree with at least two vertices has at least two leaves.</p>

<p>Removing a leaf and its incident edge from a tree produces another
tree.</p>

<p>This property makes induction particularly natural for tree proofs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-forests">
<title>Forests</title>
<p>A forest is an acyclic graph.</p>

<p>Each connected component of a forest is a tree.</p>

<p>If a forest has <m>n</m> vertices and <m>c</m> connected components, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|
=
n-c.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-spanning-trees">
<title>Spanning Trees</title>
<definition>
<title>Spanning Tree</title>
<statement>
<p>A spanning tree of a connected graph <m>G</m> is a subgraph <m>T</m> such that:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><m>V(T)=V(G)</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>T</m> is a tree.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A spanning tree preserves connectivity while using the minimum possible
number of edges.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-connected-spanning-tree">
<title>Every Connected Graph Has a Spanning Tree</title>
<p>Take a connected graph.</p>

<p>If it contains a cycle, remove one edge from that cycle.</p>

<p>Connectivity is preserved.</p>

<p>Repeat until no cycles remain.</p>

<p>The resulting spanning subgraph is connected and acyclic.</p>

<p>Therefore it is a spanning tree.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-cayleys-formula">
<title>Cayley's Formula</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Cayley's Formula]
The number of labeled trees on <m>n</m> vertices is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
n^{n-2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>For example, the number of labeled trees on <m>4</m> vertices is</p>

<p>
<md>4^{4-2}=16.</md>
</p>

<p>One classical proof uses Prüfer codes.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-prufer-codes">
<title>Prüfer Codes</title>
<p>A labeled tree on vertex set</p>

<p>
<md>[n]</md>
</p>

<p>can be encoded by a sequence of length</p>

<p>
<md>n-2.</md>
</p>

<p>Each sequence entry belongs to <m>[n]</m>.</p>

<p>Thus there are</p>

<p>
<md>n^{n-2}</md>
</p>

<p>possible Prüfer sequences.</p>

<p>The Prüfer correspondence is bijective, giving Cayley's formula.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-rooted-trees">
<title>Rooted Trees</title>
<p>A rooted tree has one distinguished vertex called the root.</p>

<p>This creates hierarchical language such as:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>parent;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>child;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>ancestor;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>descendant;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>depth;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>subtree.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Rooted trees are fundamental in computer science and recursive
combinatorics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-euler-trails">
<title>Euler Trails</title>
<p>An Euler trail uses every edge exactly once.</p>

<p>An Euler circuit is an Euler trail that begins and ends at the same
vertex.</p>

<p>These questions were historically central to the origins of graph
theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-euler-circuit-criterion">
<title>Euler Circuit Criterion</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Euler Circuit Criterion]
A connected graph has an Euler circuit if and only if every vertex has
even degree.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Euler circuit}
\iff
\text{all degrees even}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-euler-trail-criterion">
<title>Euler Trail Criterion</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Euler Trail Criterion]
A connected graph has an Euler trail but no Euler circuit if and only if
exactly two vertices have odd degree.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>The trail begins at one odd-degree vertex and ends at the other.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-worked-euler-trail">
<title>Worked Example: Euler Trail</title>
<example>
<title>Checking Euler Structure</title>
<p>Suppose a connected graph has degrees</p>

<p>
<md>4,4,2,2,3,3.</md>
</p>

<p>Exactly two vertices have odd degree.</p>

<p>Therefore the graph has an Euler trail.</p>

<p>Because not every degree is even, it does not have an Euler circuit.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Euler trail: yes}
\qquad
\text{Euler circuit: no}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-hamiltonian-paths-cycles">
<title>Hamiltonian Paths and Cycles</title>
<p>A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once.</p>

<p>A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once and returns to the
starting vertex.</p>

<p>Unlike Euler problems, Hamiltonian problems concern vertices rather than
edges.</p>

<p>There is no simple degree characterization comparable to Euler's
criterion.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-diracs-theorem">
<title>Dirac's Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Dirac's Theorem]
Let <m>G</m> be a simple graph on <m>n\geq3</m> vertices.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(v)\geq\frac n2
}</md>
</p>

<p>for every vertex <m>v</m>, then <m>G</m> contains a Hamiltonian cycle.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This condition is sufficient but not necessary.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-ores-theorem">
<title>Ore's Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Ore's Theorem]
Let <m>G</m> be a simple graph on <m>n\geq3</m> vertices.</p>

<p>If every pair of nonadjacent vertices <m>u,v</m> satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(u)+\deg(v)\geq n,
}</md>
</p>

<p>then <m>G</m> is Hamiltonian.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Dirac's theorem follows as a special case.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-coloring">
<title>Graph Coloring</title>
<p>A proper vertex coloring assigns colors to vertices so that adjacent
vertices receive different colors.</p>

<p>A <m>k</m>-coloring uses at most <m>k</m> colors.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-chromatic-number">
<title>Chromatic Number</title>
<definition>
<title>Chromatic Number</title>
<statement>
<p>The chromatic number of a graph <m>G</m>, denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi(G),
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the minimum number of colors required for a proper vertex coloring.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Examples include</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(K_n)=n</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(C_{2m})=2.</md>
</p>

<p>For odd cycles,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi(C_{2m+1})=3.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bipartite-coloring">
<title>Bipartite Graphs and Coloring</title>
<p>A nonempty graph is bipartite if and only if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi(G)\leq2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus bipartiteness may be interpreted either structurally through a
vertex partition or algorithmically through two-colorability.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-greedy-coloring">
<title>Greedy Coloring</title>
<p>Choose an ordering of the vertices.</p>

<p>Color them one at a time, always assigning the lowest available color.</p>

<p>If the maximum degree is</p>

<p>
<md>\Delta(G),</md>
</p>

<p>then each vertex has at most <m>\Delta(G)</m> colored neighbors.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is the basic greedy coloring bound.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-chromatic-polynomial">
<title>Chromatic Polynomial</title>
<p>The chromatic polynomial</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P_G(k)
}</md>
</p>

<p>counts proper colorings of <m>G</m> using <m>k</m> available colors.</p>

<p>For a tree <m>T</m> with <m>n</m> vertices,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P_T(k)
=
k(k-1)^{n-1}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For the complete graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P_{K_n}(k)
=
k(k-1)\cdots(k-n+1).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-deletion-contraction">
<title>Deletion--Contraction</title>
<p>For an edge <m>e</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P_G(k)
=
P_{G-e}(k)
-
P_{G/e}(k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>G-e</m> deletes <m>e</m>, while <m>G/e</m> contracts <m>e</m>.</p>

<p>This recurrence connects graph coloring with algebraic combinatorics and
matroid theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-edge-coloring">
<title>Edge Coloring</title>
<p>An edge coloring assigns colors to edges so that adjacent edges receive
different colors.</p>

<p>The edge chromatic number is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi'(G).
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is the minimum number of colors needed for a proper edge coloring.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-vizing-theorem">
<title>Vizing's Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Vizing's Theorem]
For every finite simple graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\Delta(G)
\leq
\chi'(G)
\leq
\Delta(G)+1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus every simple graph belongs to one of two edge-coloring classes.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-matchings">
<title>Matchings</title>
<definition>
<title>Matching</title>
<statement>
<p>A matching is a set of edges no two of which share a vertex.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A maximum matching is a matching having the largest possible number of
edges.</p>

<p>The matching number is often denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\nu(G).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-perfect-matchings">
<title>Perfect Matchings</title>
<p>A perfect matching covers every vertex exactly once.</p>

<p>Thus a graph with a perfect matching must have an even number of
vertices.</p>

<p>Perfect matchings model complete pairings and assignments.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bipartite-matching">
<title>Bipartite Matching</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>G=(X\cup Y,E)</md>
</p>

<p>is bipartite.</p>

<p>A matching pairs vertices from <m>X</m> with vertices from <m>Y</m>.</p>

<p>Such problems model:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>workers to jobs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>students to projects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>machines to tasks;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>flights to gates.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-halls-marriage-theorem">
<title>Hall's Marriage Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Hall's Marriage Theorem]
Let</p>

<p>
<md>G=(X\cup Y,E)</md>
</p>

<p>be bipartite.</p>

<p>There exists a matching saturating every vertex of <m>X</m> if and only if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|N(S)|\geq|S|
}</md>
</p>

<p>for every subset</p>

<p>
<md>S\subseteq X.</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Here,</p>

<p>
<md>N(S)</md>
</p>

<p>is the set of neighbors in <m>Y</m> adjacent to at least one vertex of
<m>S</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-worked-hall">
<title>Worked Example: Hall's Condition</title>
<example>
<title>Checking a Matching Condition</title>
<p>Suppose a bipartite graph has left part</p>

<p>
<md>X=\{a,b,c\}</md>
</p>

<p>and right part</p>

<p>
<md>Y=\{1,2,3,4\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Assume</p>

<p>
<md>N(\{a\})=\{1,2\},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>N(\{b\})=\{2,3\},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>N(\{c\})=\{3,4\},</md>
</p>

<p>and every larger subset has at least as many neighbors as vertices.</p>

<p>Then Hall's condition holds.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{There exists a matching saturating }X.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-vertex-covers">
<title>Vertex Covers</title>
<definition>
<title>Vertex Cover</title>
<statement>
<p>A vertex cover is a set</p>

<p>
<md>C\subseteq V</md>
</p>

<p>such that every edge has at least one endpoint in <m>C</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The minimum size of a vertex cover is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tau(G).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-independent-sets">
<title>Independent Sets</title>
<p>An independent set is a set of vertices containing no adjacent pair.</p>

<p>The maximum size of an independent set is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\alpha(G).
}</md>
</p>

<p>A set <m>C</m> is a vertex cover if and only if</p>

<p>
<md>V\setminus C</md>
</p>

<p>is independent.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\alpha(G)+\tau(G)=|V|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-konigs-theorem">
<title>K\H{o}nig's Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[K\H{o}nig's Theorem]
For every bipartite graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\nu(G)=\tau(G).
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus the maximum size of a matching equals the minimum size of a vertex
cover.</p>

<p>This is a fundamental min-max theorem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-planar-graphs">
<title>Planar Graphs</title>
<p>A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane with no edge
crossings except at common endpoints.</p>

<p>A particular crossing-free drawing is called a planar embedding.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-planar-faces">
<title>Faces of a Planar Graph</title>
<p>A planar embedding divides the plane into regions called faces.</p>

<p>One face is unbounded and is called the outer face.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f</md>
</p>

<p>denotes the number of faces, Euler's formula relates <m>f</m> to the
numbers of vertices and edges.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-eulers-planar-formula">
<title>Euler's Planar Formula</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Euler's Formula]
For a connected planar graph,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|V|-|E|+|F|=2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Here,</p>

<p>
<md>|F|</md>
</p>

<p>denotes the number of faces in a planar embedding.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-worked-euler-planar">
<title>Worked Example: Euler's Formula</title>
<example>
<title>Finding the Number of Faces</title>
<p>Suppose a connected planar graph has</p>

<p>
<md>|V|=8</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>|E|=12.</md>
</p>

<p>Euler's formula gives</p>

<p>
<md>8-12+|F|=2.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>|F|=6.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
|F|=6.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-planar-edge-bound">
<title>Edge Bound for Planar Graphs</title>
<p>For a simple connected planar graph with</p>

<p>
<md>|V|\geq3,</md>
</p>

<p>every face has boundary length at least <m>3</m>.</p>

<p>Counting edge-face incidences gives</p>

<p>
<md>3|F|\leq2|E|.</md>
</p>

<p>Using Euler's formula leads to</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|
\leq
3|V|-6.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This provides a necessary condition for planarity.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-planar-bipartite-bound">
<title>Planar Bipartite Edge Bound</title>
<p>A bipartite graph contains no triangle.</p>

<p>Thus every face of a simple planar bipartite graph has boundary length
at least <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>4|F|\leq2|E|.</md>
</p>

<p>Combining with Euler's formula yields</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|
\leq
2|V|-4.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-k5-nonplanar">
<title>Nonplanarity of <m>K_5</m></title>
<p>The complete graph <m>K_5</m> has</p>

<p>
<md>|V|=5</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>|E|=10.</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>K_5</m> were planar, the planar edge bound would require</p>

<p>
<md>10\leq3(5)-6=9,</md>
</p>

<p>which is false.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_5\text{ is nonplanar}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-k33-nonplanar">
<title>Nonplanarity of <m>K_{3,3}</m></title>
<p>The graph <m>K_{3,3}</m> is bipartite with</p>

<p>
<md>|V|=6</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>|E|=9.</md>
</p>

<p>If it were planar, the bipartite edge bound would require</p>

<p>
<md>9\leq2(6)-4=8,</md>
</p>

<p>which is impossible.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_{3,3}\text{ is nonplanar}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-kuratowskis-theorem">
<title>Kuratowski's Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Kuratowski's Theorem]
A finite graph is planar if and only if it contains no subdivision of</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_5
}</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_{3,3}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus two graphs characterize all obstructions to planarity through
subdivision.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-traversal">
<title>Graph Traversal</title>
<p>Graph traversal systematically visits vertices and edges.</p>

<p>Two fundamental algorithms are:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Breadth-First Search}
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Depth-First Search}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>They form the foundation of many graph algorithms.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-breadth-first-search">
<title>Breadth-First Search</title>
<p>Breadth-First Search, abbreviated BFS, explores vertices by increasing
distance from a starting vertex.</p>

<p>It uses a queue.</p>

<p>BFS can determine:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>connected components;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>shortest paths in unweighted graphs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>vertex distances;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>bipartiteness.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-bfs-layers">
<title>BFS Layers</title>
<p>Starting from <m>s</m>, define</p>

<p>
<md>L_0=\{s\},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_k
=
\{v:d(s,v)=k\}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>BFS discovers these layers sequentially.</p>

<p>Thus the BFS tree records shortest-path distances from <m>s</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-depth-first-search">
<title>Depth-First Search</title>
<p>Depth-First Search, abbreviated DFS, follows one branch as deeply as
possible before backtracking.</p>

<p>It uses a stack or recursion.</p>

<p>DFS can help identify:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>connected components;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>cycles;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>articulation points;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>bridges;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>topological orderings in directed acyclic graphs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-directed-acyclic-graphs">
<title>Directed Acyclic Graphs</title>
<p>A directed acyclic graph, abbreviated DAG, is a directed graph containing
no directed cycle.</p>

<p>DAGs naturally represent precedence structures.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>prerequisite networks;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>task dependencies;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>build systems;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>scheduling constraints.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-topological-ordering">
<title>Topological Ordering</title>
<p>A topological ordering of a DAG is a linear ordering of its vertices
such that every directed edge</p>

<p>
<md>u\to v</md>
</p>

<p>places <m>u</m> before <m>v</m>.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
A finite directed graph has a topological ordering if and only if it is
acyclic.
\end{theorem}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-shortest-paths">
<title>Shortest Paths</title>
<p>In an unweighted graph, BFS finds shortest paths.</p>

<p>In a weighted graph with nonnegative edge weights, Dijkstra's algorithm
finds shortest paths from one source.</p>

<p>The path length is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_{e\in P}w(e).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Shortest-path problems are fundamental in transportation and network
optimization.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-minimum-spanning-trees">
<title>Minimum Spanning Trees</title>
<p>Suppose a connected graph has weighted edges.</p>

<p>A minimum spanning tree is a spanning tree minimizing</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_{e\in T}w(e).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Two classical algorithms are:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Kruskal's algorithm;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Prim's algorithm.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Minimum spanning trees connect graph theory with greedy optimization.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-kruskals-algorithm">
<title>Kruskal's Algorithm</title>
<p>Kruskal's algorithm repeatedly selects the currently cheapest edge that
does not create a cycle.</p>

<p>The process stops after selecting</p>

<p>
<md>|V|-1</md>
</p>

<p>edges.</p>

<p>The result is a minimum spanning tree.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-prims-algorithm">
<title>Prim's Algorithm</title>
<p>Prim's algorithm grows one connected tree.</p>

<p>Starting from one vertex, repeatedly add the cheapest edge joining the
current tree to a new vertex.</p>

<p>When all vertices are included, the resulting tree is minimum-weight.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-laplacian">
<title>Graph Laplacian</title>
<p>Let <m>A</m> be the adjacency matrix and <m>D</m> the diagonal degree matrix:</p>

<p>
<md>D_{ii}=\deg(v_i).</md>
</p>

<p>The graph Laplacian is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L=D-A.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The Laplacian connects graph structure with linear algebra,
probability, geometry, and spectral theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-laplacian-connectivity">
<title>Laplacian and Connectivity</title>
<p>The vector</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{1}
=
(1,\ldots,1)^T</md>
</p>

<p>satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>L\mathbf{1}=0.</md>
</p>

<p>The multiplicity of the eigenvalue <m>0</m> equals the number of connected
components.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G\text{ connected}
\iff
0\text{ is a simple Laplacian eigenvalue}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-matrix-tree-theorem">
<title>Matrix--Tree Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Matrix--Tree Theorem]
The number of spanning trees of a finite graph <m>G</m> equals any cofactor
of its Laplacian matrix.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>That is, delete one row and the corresponding column from <m>L</m>.</p>

<p>The determinant of the resulting matrix is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tau(G),
}</md>
</p>

<p>the number of spanning trees.</p>

<p>This provides a striking connection between determinants and
combinatorial enumeration.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-network-flow-preview">
<title>Network Flow Preview</title>
<p>A flow network is a directed graph in which edges have capacities.</p>

<p>One seeks to send as much flow as possible from a source <m>s</m> to a
sink <m>t</m>.</p>

<p>The central result is the max-flow min-cut theorem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-max-flow-min-cut-preview">
<title>Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem]
In a finite capacitated network,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{maximum }s\text{-}t\text{ flow}
=
\text{minimum }s\text{-}t\text{ cut capacity}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This is another fundamental min-max principle.</p>

<p>Network flow is developed more fully in optimization and operations
research.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-products-preview">
<title>Graph Products Preview</title>
<p>Graphs may be combined through several product constructions.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Cartesian product;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>direct product;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>strong product;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>lexicographic product.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>For example, the <m>n</m>-dimensional hypercube satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Q_n
=
K_2\square K_2\square\cdots\square K_2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\square</md>
</p>

<p>denotes the Cartesian graph product.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-hypercube-graphs">
<title>Hypercube Graphs</title>
<p>The hypercube graph <m>Q_n</m> has vertex set</p>

<p>
<md>\{0,1\}^n.</md>
</p>

<p>Two binary strings are adjacent when they differ in exactly one
coordinate.</p>

<p>Thus adjacency corresponds to Hamming distance <m>1</m>.</p>

<p>The graph has</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
2^n
}</md>
</p>

<p>vertices.</p>

<p>Every vertex has degree</p>

<p>
<md>n.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E(Q_n)|
=
n2^{n-1}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-homomorphisms">
<title>Graph Homomorphisms</title>
<p>A graph homomorphism from <m>G</m> to <m>H</m> is a map</p>

<p>
<md>f:V(G)\to V(H)</md>
</p>

<p>such that adjacency is preserved:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
uv\in E(G)
\Longrightarrow
f(u)f(v)\in E(H).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Proper <m>k</m>-colorings are exactly graph homomorphisms</p>

<p>
<md>G\to K_k.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus coloring may be viewed as a structure-preserving map problem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-theory-applications">
<title>Applications</title>
<p>\begin{applicationbox}</p>

<p><em>Transportation.</em>
Roads, airline routes, shipping lanes, and transit systems are modeled
as graphs.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Computer Networks.</em>
Routers, devices, and communication links form graph structures.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Social Networks.</em>
People or organizations form vertices while relationships form edges.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Biology.</em>
Protein interactions, food webs, neural networks, and phylogenetic
relationships use graph models.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Chemistry.</em>
Molecules can be represented as graphs with atoms as vertices and bonds
as edges.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Scheduling.</em>
Precedence constraints form DAGs, while incompatibility constraints can
form coloring graphs.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Optimization.</em>
Shortest paths, spanning trees, matchings, flows, cuts, and routing are
graph optimization problems.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Data Science.</em>
Graph models describe relational, networked, and non-Euclidean data.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Probability.</em>
Random graph theory studies typical network structure and threshold
phenomena.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Algebra.</em>
Spectral graph theory uses matrices, eigenvalues, and algebraic
invariants to study graphs.</p>

<p>\end{applicationbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-theory-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-a-graph-with-its-drawing">
<title>Confusing a Graph with Its Drawing</title>
<p>Different drawings may represent the same graph.</p>

<p>Edge crossings in a drawing do not create vertices unless explicitly
declared.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-walks-trails-and-paths">
<title>Confusing Walks, Trails, and Paths</title>
<p>A walk may repeat vertices and edges.</p>

<p>A trail cannot repeat edges.</p>

<p>A path cannot repeat vertices.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-euler-and-hamiltonian-problems">
<title>Confusing Euler and Hamiltonian Problems</title>
<p>Euler problems concern using every edge.</p>

<p>Hamiltonian problems concern visiting every vertex.</p>

<p>These are fundamentally different conditions.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-degree-sum-without-dividing-by-two">
<title>Using Degree Sum Without Dividing by Two</title>
<p>Because each edge contributes to two vertex degrees,</p>

<p>
<md>|E|
=
\frac12
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v).</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-degree-sequence-is-graphical">
<title>Assuming Every Degree Sequence Is Graphical</title>
<p>A list of nonnegative integers may fail to represent any simple graph.</p>

<p>For example, degree values may violate structural constraints even when
their total sum is even.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-connected-and-complete">
<title>Confusing Connected and Complete</title>
<p>Connected means every pair is joined by some path.</p>

<p>Complete means every pair is joined directly by an edge.</p>

<p>Every complete graph is connected, but most connected graphs are not
complete.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-connected-graph-is-a-tree">
<title>Assuming Every Connected Graph Is a Tree</title>
<p>A tree must be connected and acyclic.</p>

<p>Connected graphs may contain many cycles.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-euler-s-planar-formula-for-a-nonplanar-drawing">
<title>Using Euler's Planar Formula for a Nonplanar Drawing</title>
<p>Euler's formula applies to a planar embedding.</p>

<p>A drawing with crossings is not automatically a planar embedding.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-the-planar-edge-bound-proves-planarity">
<title>Assuming the Planar Edge Bound Proves Planarity</title>
<p>The inequality</p>

<p>
<md>|E|\leq3|V|-6</md>
</p>

<p>is necessary for simple planar graphs, but it is not sufficient.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-matching-and-vertex-cover">
<title>Confusing Matching and Vertex Cover</title>
<p>A matching is a set of pairwise disjoint edges.</p>

<p>A vertex cover is a set of vertices touching every edge.</p>

<p>Their relationship is especially strong in bipartite graphs through
K\H{o}nig's theorem.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-theory-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a simple graph.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>What is the maximum number of edges in a simple graph on <m>8</m>
vertices?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the number of edges in</p>

<p>
<md>K_7.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the number of edges in</p>

<p>
<md>K_{4,6}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A graph has degree sequence</p>

<p>
<md>4,4,3,3,2,2.</md>
</p>

<p>Find the number of edges.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a graph cannot have exactly three odd-degree vertices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the degree of every vertex in</p>

<p>
<md>C_8.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the number of edges in the <m>5</m>-dimensional hypercube <m>Q_5</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a connected graph.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a tree.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>How many edges does a tree on <m>20</m> vertices have?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>How many edges does a forest with <m>20</m> vertices and <m>4</m> components
have?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Euler circuit criterion.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Euler trail criterion.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the chromatic number.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(K_6).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(C_8).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(C_7).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the handshaking lemma by double counting incidences.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that every graph has an even number of odd-degree vertices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>(3,3,2,2,2,2)</md>
</p>

<p>is graphical.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use Havel--Hakimi to test</p>

<p>
<md>(4,4,3,2,2,1).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that every tree with at least two vertices has at least two
leaves.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that every tree on <m>n</m> vertices has <m>n-1</m> edges.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that every connected graph contains a spanning tree.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the number of labeled trees on <m>5</m> vertices using Cayley's
formula.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>List all Prüfer sequences for labeled trees on <m>3</m> vertices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether a connected graph with degrees</p>

<p>
<md>4,4,2,2,2,2</md>
</p>

<p>has an Euler circuit.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether a connected graph with degrees</p>

<p>
<md>3,3,2,2,2,2</md>
</p>

<p>has an Euler trail.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that every bipartite graph contains no odd cycle.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that every tree is bipartite.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the adjacency matrix of the path graph <m>P_4</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Square the adjacency matrix of <m>P_4</m> and interpret one nonzero entry.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use Euler's planar formula to determine the number of faces in a
connected planar graph with <m>10</m> vertices and <m>15</m> edges.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the planar edge bound to prove that <m>K_5</m> is nonplanar.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the bipartite planar edge bound to prove that <m>K_{3,3}</m> is
nonplanar.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State Hall's Marriage Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give a bipartite graph satisfying Hall's condition and construct a
matching saturating the left part.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that a connected graph with <m>n</m> vertices and <m>n-1</m> edges is a
tree.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that an acyclic graph with <m>n</m> vertices and <m>n-1</m> edges is
connected.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that an edge is a bridge if and only if it belongs to no cycle.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that a graph is bipartite if and only if it contains no odd
cycle.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the formula</p>

<p>
<md>|E(Q_n)|=n2^{n-1}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that</p>

<p>
<md>P_T(k)=k(k-1)^{n-1}</md>
</p>

<p>for every tree <m>T</m> on <m>n</m> vertices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify deletion--contraction for the chromatic polynomial of <m>C_3</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1</md>
</p>

<p>using greedy coloring.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha(G)+\tau(G)=|V|.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Apply Hall's theorem to determine whether a perfect assignment exists
in a bipartite graph of your own construction.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the planar edge bound</p>

<p>
<md>|E|\leq3|V|-6.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the bipartite planar edge bound</p>

<p>
<md>|E|\leq2|V|-4.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the multiplicity of the Laplacian eigenvalue <m>0</m> equals the
number of connected components for a small example.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Matrix--Tree Theorem to compute the number of spanning trees of
<m>K_4</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use BFS to determine shortest-path distances from a chosen vertex in a
graph of your own construction.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use DFS to identify the connected components of a disconnected graph.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the Erd\H{o}s--Gallai theorem for graphical degree sequences.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate Menger's theorem and its relationship to vertex and edge
connectivity.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the Matrix--Tree Theorem and prove it using Laplacian
determinants.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate spectral graph theory and the relationship between adjacency
eigenvalues and graph structure.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study Brooks' theorem as a refinement of the greedy coloring bound.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate the Four Color Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study Tutte's theorem characterizing graphs with perfect matchings.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate K\H{o}nig's theorem and prove it using augmenting paths.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the max-flow min-cut theorem and its relationship to bipartite
matching.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate Kuratowski's theorem and Wagner's theorem on planarity.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-theory-summary">
<title>Conceptual Summary</title>
<p>A graph is a pair</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G=(V,E)
}</md>
</p>

<p>representing vertices and pairwise relationships.</p>

<p>The degree of a vertex satisfies the handshaking identity</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2|E|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Important graph classes include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_n,
\quad
K_{m,n},
\quad
C_n,
\quad
P_n,
\quad
Q_n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>A graph is connected when every pair of vertices is joined by a path.</p>

<p>A tree is a connected acyclic graph and satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|=|V|-1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>A forest with <m>c</m> components satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|=|V|-c.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Euler circuits are characterized by even vertex degrees.</p>

<p>Hamiltonian cycles visit every vertex exactly once.</p>

<p>The chromatic number</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\chi(G)
}</md>
</p>

<p>measures the minimum number of colors needed for a proper coloring.</p>

<p>Matchings pair vertices without overlap.</p>

<p>Hall's theorem characterizes when a bipartite matching can saturate one
part.</p>

<p>For bipartite graphs,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\nu(G)=\tau(G)
}</md>
</p>

<p>by K\H{o}nig's theorem.</p>

<p>Planar graphs satisfy Euler's relation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|V|-|E|+|F|=2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Simple planar graphs satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|E|\leq3|V|-6.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Adjacency matrices encode edges, and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(A^k)_{ij}
}</md>
</p>

<p>counts length-<m>k</m> walks.</p>

<p>The Laplacian</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L=D-A
}</md>
</p>

<p>encodes connectivity and spanning-tree information.</p>

<p>Graph theory therefore provides a unified mathematical language for</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{networks}
+
\text{paths}
+
\text{connectivity}
+
\text{coloring}
+
\text{matching}
+
\text{optimization}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-09-graph-theory-subsec-graph-theory-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Graph Theory builds on the discrete structures introduced in
Section <xref ref="sec-discrete-structures" /> and uses the counting,
enumerative, extremal, algebraic, and probabilistic methods developed
throughout Chapter 6. Extremal Combinatorics studies edge bounds under
forbidden-subgraph conditions. Algebraic Combinatorics contributes
chromatic polynomials, spectra, group actions, and the Tutte polynomial.
Probabilistic Combinatorics studies random graphs and typical graph
behavior. The next section, Ramsey Theory, asks when large graphs or
edge colorings must contain unavoidable configurations. Design Theory
uses highly regular incidence graphs, while Matroid Theory generalizes
trees, independence, rank, circuits, and deletion--contraction.
Optimization and Operations Research develops shortest paths, flows,
matchings, network optimization, and spanning-tree methods in greater
algorithmic depth.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
